  "text": "Danu is an Irish goddess. She is also called Dana, Anu, or Danann. No myths about her survive. Yet many place-names bear her name. The Dane Hills carry her name. The river Danube in eastern Europe carries it. The Paps of Danu stand in Munster. These are two breast-shaped hills. Prehistoric cairns top them like nipples. The Tuatha Dé Danann (Tuatha De Danann) take their name from her. This name means the people of the goddess Danu. Her name descends from Old Celtic dán, meaning knowledge. Some link her to Dôn, the Welsh mother goddess Don. Some texts call her daughter of Dagda. This link supports her fertility. Some equate her with Danann, daughter of Flidais. Others call her mother of the Sons of Tuireann.",
  "objective": "This record documents the Irish goddess Danu, her namesake places, and her divine descendants.",
